A robot drives through a scene. It knows roughly how far it moved between frames (odometry), and
now and then it sees a landmark. We want the most probable trajectory and landmark positions given
everything it measured. This is the problem at the heart of SLAM and bundle adjustment, and it
exercises most of what makes skoro different:

- the poses live on $\SE$, a curved manifold, and every update is a retraction;
- every term is Gaussian, so the model already knows its own residuals and Jacobian structure;
- the objective's structure is read back from the model, not written a second time.

## The model

Poses $\pose_k \in \SE$, landmarks $\lm_j \in \R^3$, odometry $\odom_k$ and observations
$\meas_{kj}$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\pose_0 &\sim \NSE(\bar\pose_0,\ P_0), \\
\pose_{k+1} \mid \pose_k &\sim \NSE(\pose_k \circ \odom_k,\ Q), \\
\meas_{kj} \mid \pose_k, \lm_j &\sim \calN(\pose_k^{-1} \cdot \lm_j,\ R),
\end{aligned}
$$

where $\NSE(\mu, \Sigma)$ means that $\pose \ominus \mu$ is $\calN(0, \Sigma)$ in the tangent space
at $\mu$. That is exactly what `Normal<B, SE3>` is.

