## The objective

The MAP estimate maximizes the log-posterior. Every term is Gaussian, so this is a nonlinear least
squares problem in the terms' whitened residuals:

$$
\hat\pose, \hat\lm
= \arg\max_{\pose, \lm} \log p(\pose, \lm \mid \meas)
= \arg\min_{\pose, \lm} \tfrac12 \sum_t \lVert r_t \rVert^2 .
$$

Every `Normal` term has already bound its residual $r_t$ under `RESIDUAL`, so the problem's rows can
be read straight off the model.

## Solving it

One damped Gauss–Newton (Levenberg–Marquardt) step, which is also Fisher scoring for this model
([why](../../concepts/fisher-scoring/)):

1. `tangent_layout(&ids)` for the columns: 6 per pose and 3 per landmark.
2. `linearize(&model, points, &free, Curvature::Residual)`: one factor per term, $J_t \delta \approx -r_t$,
   each over only the poses and landmarks it touches. It checks `require_role(&model, RESIDUAL)`
   first.
3. On the host: `graph.damped(λ)` appends $\sqrt\lambda I$ per key, a `JunctionTree` orders it by
   COLAMD once, and `eliminate(...).tree.back_substitute()` solves
   $\begin{bmatrix} J \\ \sqrt\lambda I \end{bmatrix}\delta = \begin{bmatrix} -r \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}$
   block-sparsely — the cost grows with the map's connectivity, not its size squared.
4. `to_flat_tensor` turns the per-key step back into a tensor over the layout.
5. `retract_flat`: each pose moves by $\pose \leftarrow \pose \circ \exp(\delta)$, and each landmark by addition.
6. Accept the step and shrink $\lambda$ if $\log p$ rose; otherwise reject it and grow $\lambda$.

